WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.440
Nowadays, it isn't unusual for a big-screen
blockbuster to run well over two hours, but

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.380
even with lengthier run-times, filmmakers
aren't completely free to include every scene

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:10.940
they may envision for the final product.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.120
Scenes end up on the cutting room floor of
every blockbuster — and others are scrapped

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.120
before they're even filmed.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:20.180
While the theatrical film is often all the
better for the cuts, it's always interesting

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:21.500
to hear about what might have been.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.720
In the case of Black Panther, most fans certainly
had no complaints regarding the theatrical

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.220
cut, which had a 134-minute runtime.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.560
Still, we might have gotten even more.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.060
Here are the Black Panther scenes you didn't
see.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.640
T'Challa and Nakia go for a run

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.860
Director and co-writer Ryan Coogler said the
movie constantly changed throughout filming,

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.980
ultimately resulting in the loss of some potentially
important moments.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.960
One such moment would have given viewers a
better glimpse at Wakanda through the eyes

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.720
of T'Challa and Nakia as the pair went for
a run.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.560
Coogler told Digital Spy:

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.540
"I was so in love with Wakanda, I wanted to
explore it more."

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.280
In addition to revealing more of the secretive
nation, the scene would have also introduced

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.190
some important new characters — namely,
the parents of Nakia, whom the pair would

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.560
have met at her childhood home at the end
of their journey.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:06.620
Coogler explained:

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:11.360
"Think of it as a Rocky-style jog or a Muhammad
Ali When We Were Kings-style jog."

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.300
However, the scene had to be cut early on
due to budgetary constraints.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.100
Coogler said he was ultimately okay with the
fact that they never got to shoot the scene,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.560
considering it likely would have been cut
for time anyway.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.700
Still, it's sad to hear that viewers won't
have a chance to get this extra glimpse of

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.340
Wakanda in any DVD extras.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.680
There's always room for some run action in
the sequel, though.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.800
Okoye and Ayo flirt

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:37.900
Black Panther has been hailed for its representation
of minority characters, but one cut scene

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.060
has caused controversy among some communities.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:45.020
The scene reportedly featured Danai Gurira's
Okoyeflirting with Florence Kasumba's Ayo,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.200
another member of the Dora Milaje.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.300
Vanity Fair first called attention to the
scene in April of 2017, when writer Joanna

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.700
Robinson said she'd seen a cut of a scene
in which the Dora Milaje were dancing together.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:02.240
In the scene, Okoye reportedly looks flirtatiously
at Ayo, telling her, "You look good."

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.760
When Ayo tells her the same thing, Okoye smiles
and says, "I know."

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:12.060
Many were hopeful that the scene was an indication
that the movie would feature the LGBT storyline

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:16.780
from the comic World of Wakanda, but Marvel
later crushed those hopes by saying the comic

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:18.800
wasn't used as source material for the film.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:24.400
Instead, Gurira's Okoye is depicted in a relationship
with Daniel Kaluuya's W'Kabi.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.820
Black Panther co-writer Joe Robert Cole was
vague in an interview when asked about the

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.480
potential storyline, saying he didn't remember
the scene in question.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.060
But he did add that:

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.180
"[There were] a few conversations around different
things, different directions with different

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.440
characters, and characters that we may have."

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.700
Kasumba herself later spoke out on the matter,
seeming to say that the scene had existed,

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:45.780
revealing to Vulture:

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.020
"There were a few scenes that have been cut.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:51.820
[...] Whatever their reason is, I can't tell you,
because nobody told me about whether it's

00:02:51.829 --> 00:02:52.400
in or not."

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.220
Perhaps that scene will make it into the home
video extras to make up for the missed opportunity.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.900
Thanks for watching!

